https://brcaexchange.org/variant/788630,V=39=10.0,K=40=http://bioconductor.org/packages/release/data/experiment/html/msigdb.html,V=40=10.0,K=41=https://toolshed.g2.bx.psu.edu,V=41=10.0,K=42=https://bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/html/cBioPortalData.html,V=42=10.0,K=43=https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/32412846/,V=43=10.0,K=44=https://oncomx.org/faq?fq=/static/docs/UpdatedREADME.1.0.12.txt,V=44=10.0,K=45=https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/BiocFHIR,V=45=10.0,K=46=TOPAS connects with 3D Slicer through DICOM RT. The file format of DICOM RT is outside of both of these tools and is well specified. In the case of connectivity between tools that can read and write DICOM-RT,, the evidence is trivial – we wrote out a DICOM RT file from TOPAS and viewed it in 3D Slicer. All of the information for creating DICOM RT with TOPAS and/or 3D Slicer and reading it with 3D Slicer and/or TOPAS is available to users of these two tools. We don’t see there’s really any evidence to provide,, other than to say we tried it and it works!,V=46=10.0,K=47=https://software.broadinstitute.org/software/igv/book/export/html/6,V=47=10.0,K=48=https://run.opencravat.org/submit/nocache/index.html,V=48=10.0,K=49=https://fets-ai.github.io/Front-End/itcr_connectivity#CaPTk,V=49=10.0,K=50=https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/RGalaxy/,V=50=10.0,K=51=https://webmev.tm4.org/#/about,V=51=10.0,K=52=https://github.com/igvteam/igv.js/blob/2c21e74613cace56852e7561dcc6a0cdb5126e55/js/civic/civicReader.js,V=52=10.0,K=53=https://civic.readthedocs.io/en/latest/about/data-clients.html,V=53=10.0,K=54=https://www.gsea-msigdb.org/gsea/msigdb/annotate.jsp,V=54=10.0,K=55=https://cancer.usegalaxy.org/,V=55=10.0,K=56=https://vjcitn.github.io/BiocOpenCRAVAT/articles/BiocOpenCRAVAT.html,V=56=10.0,K=57=https://cbica.github.io/CaPTk/ITCR_Connectivity.html#itcr_dcmtk,V=57=10.0,K=58=https://drive.google.com/file/d/1L67mMfZdLIlmzWE_tdqcITl4nAxDd03m/view?usp=sharing,V=58=10.0,K=59=https://www.cancermodels.org/validation/valid